  "findings": [
    "Minimum evidence that fails the documented rule: at n=3 even a perfect 3/3 record gives Wilson LB=0.4385 < threshold 0.60, so the 06 rule (N_min=3 + delta=0.10) cannot promote at its own minimum sample. The rule is missing an evaluation horizon.",
    "At n=8 the documented rule promotes only perfect records: null false-promotion 0.42%, retention at p=0.90 42.75%. Minimum k to pass at n=8 is 8/8.",
    "Count-only N=3 (the rule without the Wilson guard) promotes a 50%-reliable candidate 12.21% of the time; the calibrated variant (n=8, k>=7, LB>p0) reduces null promotions to 3.51% while retaining 81.14% of p=0.90 candidates.",
    "Demotion: '2 failures in last 20' fires on a healthy 95%-reliable skill 41.22% of the time over 30 uses. '3 of 20' lowers this to 15.10%; '2 consecutive' is 6.88%; CUSUM(k=0.15,h=1.0) is 22.98% with median detection 2.0 uses after drift.",
    "Regression gate: a 40-golden suite with a 5%-per-golden regression misses 19.48% of risky candidates (promoted unseen); 80 goldens miss 3.87%; at 2% per-golden it still misses 51.95% with 40 goldens.",
    "Reward hacking: the gate-optimising strategy is accepted and promoted 85.73% of the time when the human check is off, and 0.01% with it on. The gate/human disagreement rate separates strategies: honest 8.02% vs gate_optimiser 90.23%.",
    "Null-result A/B: identical arms (p=0.70) are declared 'improved' by a naive higher-rate comparison 33.06% of the time at n=3 and 47.17% at n=100; the two-sided z-test flags 1.78% and 4.89% respectively. A single small run is not evidence.",
    "Power: detecting a real 0.70 vs 0.85 difference needs about 100 per arm for 72.75% detection at alpha=0.05 in this model."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "All probabilities are assumed inputs; results are arithmetic consequences of the model.",
    "No Craft artifact was produced or measured; this is not evidence about design quality.",
    "Gate reliability, human-rater behaviour and real skill drift are not modelled.",
    "The 'calibrated' variant is a simulation-derived suggestion, not an accepted policy."
